/-
Johnson/G/SendsWritten.lean: the lists of sendings of oracle messages of the key file's and the
proof file's three chains each, at the full size, written out in the very type in which they are
made (`ChainSends`, `Sending`, Johnson/G/Sends.lean): 114 sendings. Definitions alone; data.

What it is for. `sendsOf` (Johnson/G/Sends.lean) makes these lists from the public files by parsing
and by a walk over all rounds, which Lean's kernel does not evaluate at the full size. A test in the
verifier holds the made lists to these (Johnson/Desc/Ok/PlanWritten/SendsTest.lean); facts on the
written lists are small enough for the kernel.

  `keyCodesWritten`, `proofCodesWritten`: the two files' codes.
  `keySends0`, `keySends1`, `keySends2`: 6, 9 and 4 sendings. `proofSends0`, `proofSends1`,
    `proofSends2`: 82, 9 and 4.
  `keySendsFull`, `proofSendsFull`: each file's three.
A sending's five fields: the number of its entry "SEND seed" among the chain's rounds, from 1; its
label; the oracle messages that are sent, each a shape with its class of leaves; that entry; the
entry behind it.

Whence the values. A program laid this text off ONE PRINT that Lean's evaluator made of the fields
`made.key` and `made.proof` of the public part of the IOP as written in Lean, at the full size's
three public files. ON HOW MANY PRINTS EACH PART STANDS: the file, the chain, the entry's number,
what is sent, and the codes stand on two prints: Lean's kernel holds them against the library's
older written values `sendsFullLit` (Johnson/G/SendsLit.lean) and `codesFull0`, `codesFull2`
(Johnson/G/PackLit.lean), which were laid off another print (`written_lit`, `written_codes`,
Johnson/Desc/Ok/PlanWritten/SendsTestLaws.lean). THE LABELS AND THE TWO ENTRIES OF EACH SENDING REST
ON THE ONE PRINT, and on one test by Lean's evaluator at every build, which makes the six lists anew
by `sendsOf` and compares them whole (Johnson/G/SendsWrittenGuard.lean: no theorem; its head says
how far print and test are apart). A wrong value here can make the test reject honest files; it
cannot make it accept lists other than these.

Limits. No theorem is stated. The list of the copy's file's one chain (five sendings) is not here:
the structure that keeps the made lists keeps six of the seven. Nothing on soundness.
-/
